---
Fix Reset-to-Defaults wiping the kit on Linux AppImage — diagnosis
confirmed via stderr capture from v1.3.0 AppImage launched from Nautilus.

## Root cause (confirmed)

Autosave at ~/.local/share/0x808/autosave.sqproj contained:
    "sample_paths": ["", "", "", "", "", "", "", ""]
producing 8x "Rejected unsafe sample path:" warnings on next load.

Failure cycle:
1. An initial launch (likely first Nautilus double-click with CWD=$HOME)
   triggered sq_kit_load (kits.c:134) which memsets the 8 sample slots
   (kits.c:154-155) BEFORE attempting load. All loads failed, leaving
   8 zeroed slots — filepath="" AND name="".
2. project_save (project.c:316-322) wrote those empty strings to JSON
   without guarding against filepath="" && name="".
3. Subsequent project_load rejected every entry via path_is_safe (which
   returns false for empty strings, project.c:39), leaving
   engine->num_samples = 0 after load.
4. Startup kit-restore in main_gui.c:624-635 only re-runs sq_kit_load
   if `need_reload || sq_current_kit > 0`. With num_samples=0 the
   need_reload loop doesn't execute, and if sq_current_kit==0 the
   fallback is skipped — so the engine boots with zero samples.
5. User clicks Reset to Defaults: settings_panel.cpp:486 reads
   ns=engine->num_samples (=0), rebuilds each pattern with
   num_tracks = 0 + 2 (synth tracks only, NO sampler rows). Then
   sq_kit_load fills sample slots 0..7 successfully, but patterns
   no longer have any sampler tracks pointing at them.
   → Kit dropdown looks empty, every kit selection appears to do
   nothing because there are no sampler rows in the grid.

Manual recovery: delete ~/.local/share/0x808/{autosave.sqproj,session.json}.

## Required fixes

Three independent guards — all three should land:

1. **Prevent the bad-state save** (project.c:316-322)
   Skip serialising sample slots where both filepath and name are
   empty. Better: if a slot has data == NULL, don't write it at all
   (it was never loaded). This prevents the autosave from poisoning
   future launches.

2. **Self-heal on load** (project.c:695-804 and/or main_gui.c:624-635)
   After project_load, if engine->num_samples ended up smaller than
   the JSON's sample_paths array length (i.e. entries were rejected),
   force a sq_kit_load(engine, sq_current_kit, base_dir) regardless
   of sq_current_kit's value. Rationale: the autosave referenced
   samples we couldn't restore, so fall back to the named kit.

3. **Fix the Reset-to-Defaults order** (settings_panel.cpp:479-548)
   Move `sq_kit_load(engine, 0, engine->base_dir)` BEFORE the
   pattern rebuild loop, so `ns = engine->num_samples` reads the
   freshly-loaded slot count. Today the order is reversed and a
   zero-sample state propagates into the rebuilt patterns.

Bonus: add a LOG_INFO at reset entry dumping engine->base_dir and
engine->num_samples — would have caught this in one log line.

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] project_save no longer writes "" entries to sample_paths
- [ ] project_load that rejects entries triggers a kit reload
      so num_samples is non-zero on success
- [ ] Reset-to-Defaults rebuilds patterns AFTER kit load
- [ ] Reproduce: hand-craft an autosave with empty sample_paths,
      launch app, verify samples are restored from default kit
      instead of starting in the broken zero-sample state
- [ ] Reset-to-Defaults from the broken state recovers the kit
- [ ] Apply equivalent fixes to GTK frontend (frontend parity)

## Test Plan
- [ ] Reproduce the original cycle: write `"sample_paths":["","",...]`
      to autosave.sqproj manually, launch AppImage, confirm pre-fix
      shows zero kit and post-fix recovers
- [ ] Verify normal launch (with valid autosave) is unaffected
- [ ] Verify Windows installer + macOS .app reset still work
- [ ] Add a project_load unit test: feed JSON with 8 empty
      sample_paths and assert num_samples > 0 after load (via
      kit fallback)

## Notes
- The original Nautilus-launch trigger that produced the empty
  paths in the first place is a separate, lower-priority question.
  It may have been a transient base_dir issue from before the
  0ec71af project_load base_dir-preservation fix. The three guards
  above prevent a single bad save from becoming a permanent
  broken state regardless of how it occurred.
